Fred Runtime owns the immutable Alembic history for session_history and the
runtime activity/steering tables. The packaged migration runner is
`python -m fred_runtime migrate`; it also walks installed capability trees.
Use the owning application's configured migration command for deployment.

Execution-space schema (revision a7b8c9d0e1f2):
- runtime_turn_steering.team_id already contained an admitted space. It is renamed
  to space_id, preserving that value and the mailbox's identity/lease lifecycle.
- session_history.team_id contained team ancestry. It is dropped; a nullable
  space_id and its index are added. Existing rows receive NULL, never a copied
  team ID. New managed turns write their admitted space. Downgrade likewise does
  not reconstruct ancestry from space. Session/rank keys are unchanged.

PostgreSQL conversation ownership (revision b8c9d0e1f2a3):
- Control Plane session_metadata is the lifecycle root. Runtime history, steering,
  and all five v2_ checkpoint tables have generated UUID conversation_id columns
  and immediate, validated ON DELETE CASCADE foreign keys to that root. Derived
  Graph threads use the UUID before their first colon; checkpoint namespaces do
  not change ownership. The writable_document capability applies the same relation
  through its own w2r3i4t5a6b7 migration. Web research activity remains an independent
  restricted trace with its existing retention; conversation deletion does not erase it.
- Initialize the Control Plane schema and its narrow REFERENCES grant first.
  fred_runtime owns runtime tables but cannot read or create Control Plane roots.
  Control Plane deletes the root; PostgreSQL performs cascades without giving it
  direct access to runtime content. A concurrent insert either commits before
  root deletion and is cascaded, or fails its FK after deletion. Old admitted SQL
  producers cannot recreate content after a crash or on another replica.
- This migration adopts the existing v2_ checkpoint tables and creates them on a
  fresh installation. PostgreSQL startup verifies generated expressions and the
  validated, nondeferrable cascade constraints; it no longer creates or repairs
  checkpoint tables. A remaining writable_document table must also have its
  ownership migration even when the capability is no longer installed; startup
  refuses an unmigrated table rather than promising an incomplete cascade.
  The previously supported execution-space columns remain a
  prerequisite. Custom PostgreSQL prefixes are not part of the Weaver schema.
- Backfill is strict: invalid UUIDs, missing conversation roots or incompatible
  existing tables fail the whole migration. No row is silently removed, and no
  missing root is manufactured from content. Resolve such data through an explicit
  operator decision before retrying; never rewrite applied migration history.
- SQLite remains a standalone SDK/test store. Its checkpoint tables still initialize
  locally, and these cross-service FK migrations are no-ops. It does not implement
  the Weaver physical-purge endpoint. PostgreSQL ownership migrations deliberately
  refuse downgrade, which would restore the write-after-purge race.

Object filesystem coordination belongs to revision c9d0e1f2a3b4: a manifest and
pending write intents restrict root deletion until filesystem erasure is acknowledged.
The runtime's SQL content itself needs no second tombstone, write guard or purge loop.

The owner index remains retention metadata. Conversation access still uses the
existing Weaver binding_json provenance and Control Plane checks. Neither NULL
nor a supplied space_id creates missing provenance or grants access.
